# 📈 Roadmap

The current implementation focuses on the fundamental mechanics of HTTP and TCP communication.

Potential future improvements include:

* [ ] Support for additional HTTP methods.
* [ ] More complete HTTP request parsing.
* [ ] Improved HTTP status code handling.
* [ ] Custom `404 Not Found` responses.
* [ ] Path traversal protection.
* [ ] Configurable host and port.
* [ ] `Content-Length` support.
* [ ] Persistent HTTP connections.
* [ ] Concurrent client handling.
* [ ] Thread-based request processing.
* [ ] Structured logging.
* [ ] Unit and integration testing.
* [ ] Configuration management.

---

# 🎯 Project Goals

This project is not intended to replace production-grade web servers.

Its purpose is to understand what happens beneath high-level frameworks.

Instead of:

```text
🌐 Browser
    ↓
⚡ Framework
    ↓
🖥️ HTTP Server
```

This project explores:

```text
🌐 Browser
    ↓
📡 TCP Connection
    ↓
🔌 Socket
    ↓
📨 Raw HTTP Request
    ↓
🧩 Manual Parsing
    ↓
📂 Filesystem
    ↓
📨 HTTP Response
    ↓
📡 TCP Transmission
```

---

# 📚 Key Takeaways

This project provided practical experience with the relationship between:

```text
┌───────────────────────┐
│   Application Layer   │
│       HTTP/1.1        │
└───────────┬───────────┘
            ↓
┌───────────────────────┐
│   Transport Layer     │
│         TCP           │
└───────────┬───────────┘
            ↓
┌───────────────────────┐
│   Operating System    │
│       Sockets         │
└───────────┬───────────┘
            ↓
┌───────────────────────┐
│      File System      │
└───────────────────────┘
```

The project demonstrates how frameworks such as Flask, FastAPI, and Django abstract many of the low-level operations involved in receiving, processing, and responding to network requests.

